Pope Francis is summoning more than a  hundred bishops from across the world  for an unprecedented summit on  preventing clergy sex abuse the Vatican  hopes that Thursday's meeting will be a  turning point in efforts to protect  minors the Pope is face growing  criticism for his handling of the  church's sexual abuse crisis Seth Doane  is following the summit preparations is  outside the Vatican with more on this  story Seth good morning good morning  this summit is the first of its kind and  as part of the catholic church's  response to clerical sex abuse scandals  that have rocked the church from chile  to Australia to the United States so  Pope Francis is inviting the heads of  Bishops conferences from around the  world to the Vatican to try to  standardize the church's response to the  sexual abuse of minors one of the key  issues abuse survivors are hoping to see  progress on this week is holding bishops  accountable right now there are  protocols for punishing predator priests  but the higher level bishops can only be  investigated if authorized by the Pope  that is a loophole many here would like  to see closed what the US Church will  gain from this is a big question as  guidelines to protect minors have  already been in place since 2002 and  abuse survivors are playing a limited  role only 10 of them about 10 of them  will meet with organizers tomorrow on  the eve of the summit the organizers of  this summit have already been  downplaying the expectations of what can  be achieved this week saying that  follow-up is key Nora  that is true the fall will be watching  that closely Seth Doane in Rome thank  you
 

